A||||15||fierocd||Mass air flow sensor||||1||
Z||000000||fierocd||08-25-2001||10:33 PM||||Where is the Mass air flow sensor on the 2.8?  I can't find anything about it in my Chilton manual except for the trouble codes, which my car is coming up with(33).||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||08-25-2001||10:42 PM||||There is no Mass Airflow sensor on a Fiero V6.  There's a MAT sensor (air temp) in the side of the air cleaner canister and a MAP sensor (air pressure) on the right hand side of the intake plenum (is the black square box on the side of the red plenum).  The ECM uses temp and pressure to calculate mass airflow.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierocd||08-25-2001||10:49 PM||||Thats what I found when I did a search but wasn't sure if that was correct or not.  So does that mean that if either one goes bad the ECM will show a code?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierocd||08-25-2001||10:56 PM||||One more thing, my car seamed like it didn't have as much power as it should, especially pulling out, could the MAT/MAP sensors cause this if one was bad?||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||08-25-2001||11:04 PM||||Code 33 means the ECM reading low vacuum from the MAP (manifold air pressure) sensor. It's located on the end of the intake plenum near the coolant fill. It will have 3 wires and a vacuum line running to it. <P>- check the vacuum line for leaks<BR>- check the wire connection<P>It can be caused by a bad vacuum leak elsewhere. Does it seem to be idling rough or fast?<P>It could actually be a bad sensor too.<P>Without the sensor working right, the ECM will calculate some default value for vacuum and performance will suffer.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierocd||08-25-2001||11:23 PM||||It seemed like it was idiling fine.  Maybe a little high, about 1100-1200, but it also wasn't warmed up so it might go down a little more.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierocd||08-27-2001||03:26 PM||||OK, I tried switching MAP sensors(had a spare used one) the car does the same thing.  After driving for about 5 miles it backfires really loud, puffs out some thick balck smoke, then dies.  It will start right up agin but does the same thing 1-2 miles down the road.  My car won't idle by itself once it warms up and it doesn't have any power pulling out.  Any ideas??? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||08-27-2001||03:30 PM||||Get a Chilton's manual (or Haynes or preferably a factory service manual) and start with the troubleshooting chart.  First thing to check is standard tune up items.  Make sure you have good plugs, wires, and no vacuum leaks.  Check the condition of your current plugs when you pull them out.  They may tell you something aboiut the condition of the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierocd||08-27-2001||04:46 PM||||I have a Chiton manual, that isn't helping me much.  I just replaced all of the basic things like cap, rotor, coil, plugs, wires, and a bunch of other things that I can't rebember right now.  The car seems to run fine until it warms up, then it starts backfiring and puffing out black smoke with every backfire.  It also has very little power pulling out all the time.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Loki||08-27-2001||05:10 PM||||I would bet money on the module for the back firing and not running for a few miles....How long does it take for it to start doing it? A bit? 20+ miles? Mine was bad and was doing almost the same thing.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/loki-mg/loki_sig.jpg">  <BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>http://members.home.net/loki-mg||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierocd||08-27-2001||05:55 PM||||It starts doing it after about 5-10 miles.  What modual? MAP?  Is there any way to test the MAP sensor?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Joe Torma||08-27-2001||06:30 PM||||There are a things not relating to a particular sensors error code that will set it.  I don't have my manual with me so I don't remember exactly....<P>Check your TPS..I think that will help set a code 33.  <P>Check to see if your car goes into closed loop mode also....<BR>  Start the car with a jumper on the ADL connector pins.  Fast flash means open loop, slow flash means closed.  Watch it while you drive.  If you step on the gas while in closed loop, the light will stay on longer and if you decelerate it will stay out--this shows the amount of fuel added to the mixture.  I've seen cars that can't get into closed loop without setting codes(I'll let you know what to check if it won't go).<P>Joe<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||fierocd||08-27-2001||06:51 PM||||It went into closed loop after about 30 seconds.  When I drove it and stepped on the gas the check engine light was blinking at a constant pace and the same for when I let off the gas.  It didn't seem to stay on longer or stay off longer.  <BR>I took the TPS and IAC off my parts car and put them on and it didn't make any difference.<p>[This message has been edited by fierocd (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Joe Torma||08-27-2001||07:30 PM||||Ok, that's good.  I'll go for Loki's suggestion...the ignition module under the dist. cap.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fierocd||08-27-2001||08:33 PM||||OK, I changed the ignition module.  Now the car doesn't stall anymore, when I push in the clutch to slow down the idle drops a little, down to 500 at the most.  The car still backfires though.  It will run fine then it feels like the car actually pulls back, next thing you hear is pop, pop, pop.  If I let off the gas it runs fine then, but if I hit the gas again it starts backfireing in another 50 feet.  Any more ideas???  At least the stalling problem is fixed now!||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierocd||08-27-2001||08:49 PM||||Could a cracked EGR tube or bad EGR valve cause this?  The car runs fine now except for the backfiring after about 10 miles of driving.  If so, how can I find out if the EGR tube is cracked?||||reg||1
